From tru64-unix-managers  Mon Sep 18 13:22:44 1995
From: Barrett Flake <barrett () ra ! me ! wisc ! edu>
Date: Mon, 18 Sep 1995 13:22:44 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: PXG cards unsupported in Dig. UNIX 3.2C
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96075323306611


   This is an informative message for those having PXG
graphics boards in Digital boxes and who are considering
upgrading to Digital UNIX 3.2C.  According to a DEC
consultant, 3.2C does not support the PXG cards.  

   We have a 3000 AXP 600 with a PXG 24-plane graphics
card which was moved from an older 5000/200 Digital unix
workstation.  I tried upgrading the 3000/600 from 3.2 to
3.2C, but would get a kernel panic after  booting to
multiuser mode.  After spending a some time with DEC
consultants over the phone trying to resolve the problem,
it was suggested that I try a full install of 3.2C 
rather than the upgrade.  After the full install did not
take either, I agained called DEC and talked to yet another
consultant.  After describing the situation, the consultant
immediately asked if I had a PXG card and informed me
that support was dropped in 3.2C.  Why the other DEC
consultants I talked to weren't aware of this is 
a mystery to me, as well as to why there wasn't
anything in the upgrade documentation indicating that
there is a problem with PXG cards.

   I don't know if the problem is specific to only
some PXG cards with some OSF/1 workstations, but if
you have such a card and are considering upgrading
to 3.2C, it may not work.  

  -Barrett Flake

From tru64-unix-managers  Mon Sep 18 12:23:28 1995
From: Chris Glenister <crg () cranfield ! ac ! uk>
Date: Mon, 18 Sep 1995 12:23:28 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Allowing User Mounts on CDROM's
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96075323306609

Hello All,

My original question was,  how can I allow users to mount their own CDROM's on a 
/CDROM mount point using a script file. What I required was that the script file 
would have to have permissions on it to allow anyone to run it, however when it 
came to mounting the CDROM, this would have to be run with root privileges.



SUMMARY
-----------------
Everyone agreed that you should not set the setuid/setgid on the script file 
(chmod u+s, g+s)  as this is potentially a major security risk with script files which 
can run with superuser privileges.


What most people recommeded was to write a small 'C' program which will setup 
the sticky bit for the user to root instead and then run the script file, but you code 
the 'C' program to perform all the cdrom mounting as well. Some of the replies 
included a 'C' program which I have not had the chance to look at (Thanks Guys) 
which will provide a user interface to mount CDROMS.


I was also made aware of a utility called "sudo" which can be found at 
src.doc.ic.ac.uk via the anonymous ftp account or by using Archie to find "sudo".
This lets you do simple superuser things with scripts (check it out)


Thanks to everyone who relplied, it's great to hear from so may people, and if I 
have missed you off the list, please accept my apologies.


Cheers

Chris Glenister
SSLRC, Cranfield University


